At a previous organisation, we syndicated content from Drupal across multiple downstream platforms through API feeds. Over the years, a recurring problem: a developer misspells a field name in the CMS, say `summray` instead of `summary`. A downstream JavaScript platform consumes that API and builds around the typo. Months later, someone fixes the typo in Drupal. Now the downstream platform breaks, because it was built against the misspelled field. So we add a translation layer to preserve the old typo alongside the fix. This happens again. And again. Each time, the downstream system absorbs more of the upstream system's historical mistakes, and the mapping between them gets more fragile.

I solved this problem for years with what I variously called a "translation protocol" or a "data dictionary" — ad hoc scripts and field-mapping spreadsheets, reinvented slightly differently each time. It wasn't until I started reading Eric Evans' [*Domain-Driven Design*](https://www.domainlanguage.com/ddd/) that I discovered the pattern had a name: the [anticorruption layer](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/anti-corruption-layer). It's a deliberate translation boundary between two systems that prevents one system's model from leaking into another's. Instead of each downstream consumer adapting to whatever the upstream happens to expose (typos and all), you build an explicit layer that translates between the external model and your clean internal model. The upstream can change; your internal model stays coherent.

Knowing the pattern wouldn't have just saved time; it would have changed how I structured the integration from the start. An anticorruption layer is a design decision you make at the boundary, not a patch you apply after the damage compounds. I'd been solving a named problem with duct tape because the formalised solution lived in a tradition I'd never read.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Software engineering (DDD), content strategy, and AI context engineering have landed on structurally analogous problems around shared understanding
> - They share intellectual ancestry but developed solutions largely in parallel, and haven't been leveraging each other's lessons nearly enough
> - AI is making the resemblance visible because context failures with LLMs are fast and frequent, even when individual failures are subtle
> - If you're treating context as a domain-specific concern, you're missing transferable solutions from adjacent fields

## The pattern behind the pattern

That anticorruption layer story isn't unique to me. I [wrote recently](/posts/20260330-context-was-always-the-job/) about how context was always the job, and the more I've sat with that idea, the more I've noticed the same boundary-crossing problems showing up across fields — with solutions developed in parallel, largely without borrowing from each other.

<aside class="kh-marginnote">Linguistic anthropologists Duranti and Goodwin <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20030312200748/http://www.sscnet.ucla.edu/anthro/faculty/duranti/reprints/rethco.pdf">argued in 1992</a> that context isn't static background — it's actively produced through interaction. LLMs made this newly practical: when you prompt an agent, the context is built turn by turn, not inherited from a fixed input. The analogy has limits (an LLM operates on tokens, not mutual understanding), but the design heuristic transfers: if context must be produced through interaction, you have to design for that interaction. Paul Dourish <a href="https://www.dourish.com/publications/2004/PUC2004-context.pdf">translated this for computing in 2004</a>, but the work stayed in human-computer interaction.</aside>

**Software engineering.** Evans published DDD in 2003, arguing that the hard problem of software is shared understanding of the domain, not the code. His prescription: build a "ubiquitous language" that domain experts and developers both use, draw explicit boundaries ("bounded contexts") around different models, and build translation layers where they meet. The primary artifact is the shared model, not the running system.

**Content strategy.** Daniel Jacobson at NPR built [COPE](https://www.programmableweb.com/news/cope-create-once-publish-everywhere/2009/10/13) (Create Once, Publish Everywhere) around content modelled as structured data with standardised interfaces. Karen McGrane's [*Content Strategy for Mobile*](https://abookapart.com/products/content-strategy-for-mobile) sharpened the point: content trapped in a specific visual format can't travel across channels. You have to break it into meaningful chunks named by what they *are*, not how they *look*. "News item" survives a redesign; "featured card with sidebar layout" doesn't.

**AI context engineering.** The current wave. An LLM has general knowledge of the universe but not your project's constraints, your team's vocabulary, or why the module is shaped the way it is. The bottleneck isn't the model; it's [the context you provide](/posts/20251003-effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents/). Practitioners are building CLAUDE.md files, project briefs, scoped rules, and [structured documentation for AI consumption](/posts/20250929-ai-must-rtfm-context-curators/). The discipline is new. The problem it's solving is not.

These are the traditions I can see from where I sit — my career moved through journalism, content strategy, information architecture, design systems, and software engineering, and I now spend most of my time on AI integration. I should be honest that this is shaped by my path, not a natural taxonomy. Knowledge management has arguably the most direct claim to this territory — Nonaka & Takeuchi's tacit-to-explicit knowledge conversion framework (1995) addresses the boundary-crossing problem head-on. Library science, systems thinking, and information science all have deep roots here too. And the traditions I am covering aren't fully independent: content strategy grew out of information architecture, AI context engineering is practiced almost entirely by software engineers, DDD drew on earlier modelling traditions. They share intellectual ancestry even where they developed solutions separately.

There's a fair counterargument: maybe these traditions haven't cross-pollinated because the problems are different enough in practice that the parallels don't cash out. Evans' entities and value objects are about identity and lifecycle in domain models; McGrane's "blobs vs. chunks" is about decomposition for portability. Someone deeply embedded in either tradition would rightly push back on collapsing them. But the architectural move is the same — decompose opaque bundles into meaningful, named units, and build explicit translation at every boundary — and the anticorruption layer example shows the transfer is non-trivial, not just an interesting analogy.

The silos are institutional, not individual. Many IA practitioners have read Evans. DDD practitioners use content modelling techniques without calling them that. People move between these fields constantly — as my own career demonstrates. But the conferences, journals, and canonical texts don't overlap. A [small but growing group](https://www.jamescroft.co.uk/applying-domain-driven-design-principles-to-multi-agent-ai-systems/) is connecting DDD to AI agent design, but it hasn't reached the mainstream AI tooling ecosystem, and almost none of these voices are looking further afield to content strategy.

## Why AI is making this visible now

Before AI, context failures were slow. A misaligned contractor takes weeks to deliver the wrong thing. A content schema that doesn't match across properties causes a slow bleed of broken syndication that nobody notices until it compounds.

AI made the failure instant — but not necessarily obvious. A missing bounded context in software produces a runtime error. A missing bounded context in AI produces a plausible-sounding wrong answer. Hallucination looks like correct output. That's what makes it dangerous.

What tips the balance is volume. A misaligned contractor produces one wrong deliverable in three weeks. An LLM with bad context produces dozens of plausible-sounding wrong outputs in an afternoon. Any single failure might pass review. But when a team starts catching the same category of error repeatedly — the agent keeps confusing two domain concepts, or keeps generating content that doesn't match the schema — the pattern surfaces through repetition. When context failures were slow and rare, each tradition could treat them as domain-specific. When they're fast and frequent, the common shape becomes visible.

## A second transferred lesson

The anticorruption layer is a transfer from DDD to content architecture. Here's one running the other direction: from content strategy to AI context engineering.

Content strategists learned years ago that you define content types with required and optional fields, relationships to other types, and governance rules about who can create and edit them. This is the content schema — the structural backbone of any CMS at scale. A news article has a required headline, required body, optional teaser, required topic taxonomy, and a relationship to an author profile. These aren't suggestions; they're enforced by the CMS. And there are governance rules: editors own the content, the schema is maintained by the content architecture team, changes go through a review process.

When I started structuring context for AI agents, I recognised the same problem. An agent working on a blog post in this site's codebase needs specific context: the editorial style guide, the image path conventions (which are non-obvious — the build rewrites them), the frontmatter schema, the CSS scoping rules. Some of that context is required for every task; some is optional enrichment for specific tasks. The relationships between context sources matter: the style guide references the frontmatter spec, which references the image conventions.

A content strategist would look at this and immediately think in terms of a content schema: define the required context per task type, make the relationships explicit, assign governance for who maintains each context source. Most AI context engineering doesn't think this way yet. CLAUDE.md files tend to grow organically — someone hits a problem, adds a line, moves on. There's no schema, no required-vs-optional distinction, no governance. Content strategists have been solving this for editorial workflows for fifteen years. The scaffolding transfers directly.

## From where I sit

I'm not arguing these traditions should merge. I'm not even sure a unified theory would be useful. What I am doing is noting that the threads are visible from where I sit, and they haven't been woven together.

If you're working on context engineering for AI and you haven't read Evans on ubiquitous language, there are twenty years of lessons on formalising shared understanding that you're rediscovering from scratch. If you're doing DDD and haven't looked at how content strategists model structured content across platforms, you're missing a parallel tradition that solved the "how does this travel across boundaries" problem for editorial organisations.

The way I've come to see it: context is a verb, not a noun. It's something you actively build so that the pieces — people, systems, content, agents — can work together. None of these traditions own that insight. All of them arrived at it. The weaving is starting — but it hasn't reached mainstream practice in any of these fields yet. There's plenty left to pull together.

